# UserPrompt Service Implementation Checklist

Status: Proposed implementation plan. No production work is authorized by this
checklist until the version 1 public contract and the shared input/render
refactor boundaries in Phase 0 are approved.

Sources:

- `Docs/service-provider-api-proposal.md`
- `Docs/service-provider-api.todo`
- DwarfDirect targeting implementation in sibling checkout
  `../DwarfDirect`

## Goal

Add a typed, namespace-bound `UserPromptServiceProvider` backed by one
process-wide `UserPrompt` service. The first supported operation prompts the
user to select a location on the game map while displaying a tooltip-like title
and message below the pointer.

The service must reuse DwarfUICore's root-aware input and completed-render
transport approach. It must not add a competing DwarfDirect-style fullscreen
overlay, a second independent input-hook chain, or a normal tooltip
registration.

## Locked behavior

- [ ] The public service and provider names are `UserPrompt` and
      `UserPromptServiceProvider`; do not use `UserActionPrompt` in production
      code, public documentation, or package contracts.
- [ ] The public operation is action-specific and named
      `prompt_map_location(options)`; do not introduce a generic action enum or
      string-dispatched `prompt(kind, options)` API.
- [ ] At most one map-input consumer is active process-wide. It is active only
      while an accepted map-location prompt remains pending.
- [ ] While the map-input consumer is active, `_MOUSE_L_DOWN` is always
      consumed. Do not hit-test, validate, sample, complete, or delegate this
      boundary.
- [ ] While the map-input consumer is active, `_MOUSE_L` is always consumed.
      Synchronously sample `dfhack.gui.getMousePos()`, complete the prompt, and
      report the sampled value without map ownership, focus, tile validity,
      nil, or gesture-latch conditions.
- [ ] The completion callback accepts a detached `{x, y, z}` value or `nil`,
      matching the sampled DFHack result. Do not reinterpret `nil` as
      cancellation, rejection, or a reason to keep the prompt open.
- [ ] While the map-input consumer is active, `LEAVESCREEN` is always consumed,
      aborts the prompt, and invokes the cancellation callback when supplied.
- [ ] Right-click is not an abort boundary in version 1. Input other than
      `_MOUSE_L_DOWN`, `_MOUSE_L`, and `LEAVESCREEN` is delegated unchanged.
- [ ] Clear authoritative prompt state and hide presentation before invoking a
      completion or cancellation callback so callback code may start another
      prompt safely.
- [ ] Completion and cancellation are mutually exclusive and each accepted
      prompt invokes at most one terminal callback.
- [ ] A concurrent request is rejected with an approved stable public error;
      requests are never queued, implicitly replaced, or ordered by namespace.
- [ ] Tooltip and context-menu behavior remains unchanged when no prompt is
      active.

## Project constraints

- [ ] Preserve the current process-wide tooltip and context-menu backends.
      Shared transport extraction must not create replacement services.
- [ ] Preserve unrelated worktree and index state. Do not stash or commit while
      executing this checklist.
- [ ] Production modules start with `--@ module=true`, load project modules
      through `reqscript()`, and appear in `dwarfuicore/module_registry.lua` in
      dependency order.
- [ ] Every new or modified class and method has complete LuaDoc. Every closed
      discriminator set is an immutable numeric enum.
- [ ] Provider exports, namespace-bound APIs, opaque prompt handles, option
      snapshots, and callback-visible result values follow the existing
      immutability and runtime-generation rules.
- [ ] Provider construction is lazy and transactional. It must not reload Core,
      rescan overlays, replace healthy services, or disturb another service.
- [ ] Public APIs expose no private diagnostics, hook owners, renderer objects,
      callback references from other namespaces, or service internals.
- [ ] Unit, syntax, build, package, live automation, rendered-screen evidence,
      and cleanup are separate completion gates.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up review after every implementation phase. Do not mark
      its completion gate until the review confirms the implementation and
      focused evidence satisfy every listed requirement.

## Out of scope

- [ ] Do not add unit, item, building, area, list-choice, confirmation, text,
      quantity, or multi-location prompts in this work.
- [ ] Do not add prompt queues, priorities exposed to consumers, implicit
      replacement, timeouts, persistence, coroutine suspension, promises, or
      blocking calls.
- [ ] Do not add consumer-supplied position validation or rejection messages.
- [ ] Do not migrate DwarfDirect or another independent consumer plugin.
- [ ] Do not change DwarfDirect's targeting implementation.
- [ ] Do not make prompt text a tooltip registration or expose prompt state
      through the tooltip service API.
- [ ] Do not treat right-click as cancellation without a separately approved
      contract amendment.

## Phase 0 - Freeze the public and internal contracts

- [ ] Record branch, revision, worktree/index state, package version, module
      registry order, and relevant installed-runtime resolution before edits.
- [ ] Run and record the current complete unit, syntax, offline build, package,
      focused tooltip live, and focused context-menu live baselines.
- [ ] Amend `Docs/service-provider-api-proposal.md` so the services module
      exports exactly three immutable typed providers, adding
      `UserPromptServiceProvider` without weakening existing provider rules.
- [ ] Add `USER_PROMPT` to the private immutable numeric service-kind contract
      and approve the stable public error used for concurrent prompt rejection.
- [ ] Freeze the exact version 1 API surface:
      `get_namespace()`, `prompt_map_location(options)`, `cancel(handle)`,
      `is_active(handle)`, and `clear_namespace()`.
- [ ] Freeze the complete LuaDoc schema for map-location options, including
      required/optional title, message, source-root ownership, completion
      callback, and cancellation callback fields.
- [ ] Freeze title/message validation, copying, wrapping, empty-value behavior,
      and maximum presentation constraints before implementing the renderer.
- [ ] Freeze the callback signature so completion receives exactly one detached
      `{x, y, z}|nil` sample and cancellation receives no invented position.
- [ ] Freeze opaque handle identity fields and validation precedence across
      namespace, service kind, contract major, and runtime generation.
- [ ] Freeze `cancel`, `is_active`, and `clear_namespace` return behavior for
      current, completed, cancelled, foreign, and stale handles.
- [ ] Freeze source-root acquisition and invalidation behavior. Root loss,
      world unload, consumer cleanup, and Core reload must have an explicit
      terminal policy rather than leaving a hidden active input consumer.
- [ ] Decide and document whether non-user lifecycle termination invokes the
      public cancellation callback or silently retires the request; cover each
      termination source explicitly.
- [ ] Freeze rendering arbitration while a prompt is active: prompt content is
      rendered after ordinary content and must not be obscured by the normal
      tooltip renderer.
- [ ] Map every production/test/package file expected to be added or modified,
      including exact reload teardown and process-state owner slots.

### Phase 0 completion gate

- [ ] The proposal and this checklist agree on all public names, fields,
      callbacks, errors, nil-position behavior, lifecycle outcomes, and handle
      semantics.
- [ ] No production edit has begun before the transitive public version 1
      schema is approved.
- [ ] The follow-up review finds no unresolved behavior hidden behind phrases
      such as "valid map position", "map-owned click", or "appropriate input".

## Phase 1 - Characterize the existing shared seams

- [ ] Add or strengthen tests proving the context-menu input trampoline runs
      before its predecessor and delegates predecessor arguments and all return
      values unchanged when unhandled.
- [ ] Characterize native `plugins.overlay.feed_viewscreen_widgets` and Lua
      screen `onInput` transport selection, root reconciliation, wrapper
      adoption, foreign outer wrappers, failure containment, and reload
      retirement.
- [ ] Characterize synchronous screen/map sampling, including a nil result from
      `dfhack.gui.getMousePos()`.
- [ ] Characterize tooltip completed-render transport selection for native
      roots, overlays, and Lua screens, including draw order, pointer motion,
      resize, clamping, invalidation, and reload adoption.
- [ ] Characterize context-menu and tooltip behavior when both services are
      active so shared extraction cannot change current arbitration.
- [ ] Record the DwarfDirect evidence being adopted: an input-owning surface
      consumes by returning `true`, and input order must precede competing
      dwarfmode handlers.
- [ ] Record the DwarfDirect behavior intentionally not copied: a separate
      fullscreen overlay, action-specific targeting state, map validation,
      destination rejection, marker rendering, and right-click cancellation.

### Phase 1 completion gate

- [ ] Focused characterization tests fail under representative changes to
      pre-delegation order, handled return values, transport selection, or
      completed-render order.
- [ ] The follow-up review confirms all shared behavior needed by existing
      tooltip/context-menu services is protected before extraction.

## Phase 2 - Extract one shared input router

- [ ] Introduce reload-managed, process-owned input routing modules with one
      authoritative native trampoline and weakly owned Lua-screen trampolines.
- [ ] Preserve the existing native and screen transport enum and root
      reconciliation semantics under neutral names.
- [ ] Support internally named consumers with deterministic ordering and exact
      boolean handled results; do not expose input priorities publicly.
- [ ] Route one input event through consumers until the first returns `true`,
      then skip every later consumer and the predecessor.
- [ ] If every consumer returns `false`, invoke the predecessor exactly once
      with unchanged arguments and preserve all return values, including nils.
- [ ] Contain one consumer failure, record private diagnostics, disable or
      remove only the failed consumer as approved, and preserve safe delegation
      semantics.
- [ ] Migrate context-menu opening to the shared router without changing its
      right-click detection, root demand, open behavior, or diagnostics.
- [ ] Remove or reduce `context_menu/input_hook.lua` to an intentional adapter;
      do not leave a second live wrapper owner.
- [ ] Update reload teardown, process-owner cleanup, module registry, package
      contract, and focused diagnostics for the new shared owner.

### Phase 2 completion gate

- [ ] Existing context-menu unit and live input suites pass unchanged through
      the shared router.
- [ ] Tests prove there is exactly one active native input trampoline and no
      duplicate dispatch after construction, consumer reload, or Core reload.
- [ ] The follow-up review verifies predecessor behavior, wrapper adoption,
      failure containment, and inactive-router transparency against Phase 1.

## Phase 3 - Extract shared pointer-following presentation

- [ ] Introduce a shared completed-render transport/compositor that retains the
      tooltip presenter's native-root, overlay-root, and Lua-screen selection
      behavior.
- [ ] Preserve reload-safe render trampolines and ensure each selected owner is
      rendered through exactly one completed-render seam.
- [ ] Define internal render-layer arbitration so ordinary tooltip content and
      an active prompt cannot overwrite each other unpredictably.
- [ ] Extract or add a pointer-following message renderer supporting distinct
      title and message content, wrapping, frame/background styling, placement
      below the pointer, and window-edge clamping.
- [ ] Keep the prompt renderer input-transparent and outside tooltip
      registration, target selection, namespace collision, and contribution
      ordering.
- [ ] Migrate normal tooltip presentation to the shared transport without
      changing tooltip selection, text, placement, precedence, redraw, or
      reload behavior.
- [ ] Update teardown, module registry, package contract, and private
      diagnostics for the shared render owner.

### Phase 3 completion gate

- [ ] Existing tooltip unit and final-render DwarfSpec suites pass unchanged.
- [ ] Focused render tests prove prompt title/message placement below the mouse,
      clamping on all edges, resize relayout, motion redraw, and deterministic
      ordering relative to normal tooltips.
- [ ] The follow-up review verifies the extraction did not introduce a second
      tooltip backend, hook chain, or public renderer dependency.

## Phase 4 - Implement the private UserPrompt state machine

- [ ] Add immutable numeric internal enums for prompt state and terminal cause
      where closed discrimination is required.
- [ ] Add an immutable copied request snapshot that holds only the approved
      title, message, weak source ownership, namespace identity, and callbacks.
- [ ] Add opaque prompt handles allocated through the process identity system
      and bound to runtime generation, `USER_PROMPT`, contract major, namespace,
      and local identity.
- [ ] Enforce one process-wide pending prompt and reject concurrent requests
      before mutating state or presentation.
- [ ] Implement one idempotent terminal transition used by completion,
      cancellation, namespace cleanup, root invalidation, world unload, and
      reload retirement.
- [ ] Clear pending state, deactivate input consumption, and clear render intent
      before dispatching any terminal callback.
- [ ] Contain callback failures, report them privately, and leave the prompt
      terminal without disabling unrelated services or re-running callbacks.
- [ ] Ensure collected API objects do not cancel prompts. Apply the approved
      weak/strong lifetime contract to prompt handles and pending requests.
- [ ] Add private diagnostics for pending identity, namespace, state, selected
      root/transport, terminal counts, callback failures, input counts, and
      render counts without exposing callback values.

### Phase 4 completion gate

- [ ] Focused tests cover request acceptance, busy rejection, exact-once
      terminal transitions, reentrant callbacks, callback failure, namespace
      cleanup, root loss, world unload, and runtime retirement.
- [ ] The follow-up review confirms every termination path clears input and
      presentation before callbacks and cannot produce both outcomes.

## Phase 5 - Add the typed public provider and API

- [ ] Add `USER_PROMPT` support to service-kind validation, acquisition,
      runtime caches, identity validation, error prefixes, and provider tests.
- [ ] Add a private version 1 facade adapter that validates all arguments before
      lazy service initialization and binds the consumer namespace once.
- [ ] Add an immutable `UserPromptServiceProvider` following the existing typed
      provider construction pattern.
- [ ] Export exactly the three approved provider objects from
      `reqscript('dwarfuicore/services')`; keep `reqscript('dwarfuicore')` free
      of service APIs.
- [ ] Implement `get_namespace()`, `prompt_map_location(options)`,
      `cancel(handle)`, `is_active(handle)`, and `clear_namespace()` with the
      frozen validation, error, and return contracts.
- [ ] Copy every consumer-supplied public record before storing it and reject
      unknown fields, invalid callbacks, invalid source ownership, and malformed
      title/message fields atomically.
- [ ] Reject foreign and stale handles with the approved precedence and stable
      category tokens.
- [ ] Update provider contract, acquisition, immutability, runtime, module
      registry, command reload, and package tests for the third provider.

### Phase 5 completion gate

- [ ] Focused provider tests prove construction is distinct, immutable,
      namespace-bound, lazy, transactional, generation-safe, and non-destructive.
- [ ] Tests prove existing tooltip and context-menu APIs neither gain methods
      nor share namespace state with `UserPrompt`.
- [ ] The follow-up review compares every implementation method and public type
      against the approved version 1 schema.

## Phase 6 - Implement exact map-input consumption

- [ ] Register the UserPrompt map-input consumer with the shared router only
      while a prompt is pending and its selected input surface is active.
- [ ] On `_MOUSE_L_DOWN`, return `true` immediately. Assert through injected
      collaborators that no hit-test, map sample, terminal transition,
      callback, or predecessor invocation occurs.
- [ ] On `_MOUSE_L`, synchronously call the map sampler exactly once, return
      `true`, and complete with the copied sampled position or nil.
- [ ] Do not require a preceding `_MOUSE_L_DOWN`; `_MOUSE_L` alone completes the
      request.
- [ ] Do not latch gesture ownership or compare press/release locations.
- [ ] Do not consult native-hover ownership, pointer target/blocker detection,
      focus allowlists, map validity, tile flags, loaded blocks, reachability,
      or consumer validation.
- [ ] On `LEAVESCREEN`, return `true`, abort without sampling a position, and
      dispatch the approved cancellation behavior.
- [ ] Delegate right-click, wheel, keyboard movement, pause, and every other
      unowned input unchanged so existing consumers and the base game retain
      their established behavior.
- [ ] Define deterministic behavior for composite key tables containing more
      than one owned boundary, with terminal input precedence explicitly tested.
- [ ] Verify context-menu right-click handling remains reachable through the
      shared router while a location prompt is pending.

### Phase 6 completion gate

- [ ] Focused tests prove `_MOUSE_L_DOWN` always consumes without sampling,
      `_MOUSE_L` always consumes and completes with any sampled result including
      nil, and Esc always consumes and aborts.
- [ ] Tests prove no owned input reaches another router consumer or the
      predecessor and every unowned input retains existing delegation.
- [ ] The follow-up review searches the complete implementation for forbidden
      validation, latching, conditional consumption, and implicit right-click
      cancellation.

## Phase 7 - Integrate prompt presentation and lifecycle

- [ ] Publish prompt render intent immediately after accepting a request and
      invalidate the selected owner so title/message appear without waiting for
      unrelated pointer activity.
- [ ] Update placement from the current screen pointer through the selected
      completed-render transport while the prompt remains pending.
- [ ] Render the requested title and message in the approved tooltip-like
      layout below the pointer with deterministic clamping.
- [ ] Ensure the prompt window has no pointer ownership and cannot block or
      modify the sampled map position.
- [ ] Clear the render intent on mouse completion, Esc abort, explicit cancel,
      namespace cleanup, root invalidation, world unload, and Core reload.
- [ ] Reconcile source-root presentation changes without transferring an active
      prompt to an unrelated root.
- [ ] Add UserPrompt teardown before shared input/render owner retirement in the
      explicit Core reload order.
- [ ] Add process-state cleanup for partial initialization and failed
      construction without deleting foreign owners.

### Phase 7 completion gate

- [ ] Focused integration tests prove input and presentation activate and retire
      together on every terminal path.
- [ ] Render tests prove the final prompt is visible on supported native,
      overlay, and Lua-screen surfaces and absent after completion or abort.
- [ ] The follow-up review verifies lifecycle ordering, current-generation
      checks, and exact owner restoration under reload and failure.

## Phase 8 - Documentation, package, and automated qualification

- [ ] Document provider acquisition, map-location options, nil completion,
      exact input consumption, Esc cancellation, explicit cancellation,
      namespace cleanup, busy behavior, callback safety, and reload semantics.
- [ ] Update service-provider proposal, acceptance evidence, module registry,
      package inventory, rockspec/package version as required, and README public
      examples without exposing private modules.
- [ ] Add focused unit suites for shared input, shared render, UserPrompt state,
      provider facade, input semantics, presentation, failure, and reload.
- [ ] Add native DwarfSpec scenarios for visible prompt rendering, pointer
      following, `_MOUSE_L_DOWN` base-game suppression, `_MOUSE_L` completion,
      nil sampling seam, Esc abort, right-click delegation, callback reentry,
      world cleanup, and Core reload.
- [ ] Run focused tooltip and context-menu unit/live regressions after the shared
      refactors.
- [ ] Run the complete unit suite and complete DwarfSpec suite with terminal
      structured results and `cleanup_confirmed`.
- [ ] Run Lua syntax, offline build, expanded package verification, archive
      verification, and `git diff --check`.
- [ ] Inspect the package and installed resolution for all UserPrompt and shared
      transport modules, with no obsolete parallel hook owner packaged.

### Phase 8 completion gate

- [ ] Focused and complete automated results are recorded separately and all
      cleanup checks are terminal.
- [ ] Package contents, public documentation, LuaDoc, registry order, reload
      order, and approved contract agree exactly.
- [ ] The follow-up review finds no debug-only code, stale service names,
      unauthorized consumer migration, or unrecorded generated artifacts.

## Phase 9 - Live rendered acceptance and final review

- [ ] Install the exact verified package in the configured DFHack test
      environment and start a fresh process when installed resolution changes.
- [ ] Open a map-location prompt with distinctive title/message text and capture
      rendered-screen evidence that the complete window follows below the
      pointer and clamps at screen edges.
- [ ] Prove `_MOUSE_L_DOWN` does not reach base-game behavior while leaving the
      prompt pending.
- [ ] Prove `_MOUSE_L` consumes, reports the sampler result, removes the window,
      and does not trigger the corresponding base-game click behavior.
- [ ] Prove Esc consumes, removes the window, invokes cancellation exactly once,
      and leaves no active input consumer.
- [ ] Prove right-click and representative unowned input retain their approved
      delegated behavior during an active prompt.
- [ ] Exercise completion and cancellation callbacks that synchronously open a
      replacement prompt and verify the new request is active and visible.
- [ ] Exercise world unload, source-root loss, consumer namespace cleanup, and
      explicit Core reload; inspect prompt, input-router, render-router, service
      runtime, hook, and screen state after each.
- [ ] Run a second independent review against every locked behavior, phase gate,
      definition-of-done item, diff, and recorded evidence row.
- [ ] Remove all temporary fixtures, installed test state, active prompts,
      screens, hooks, logs requiring cleanup, and disposable registrations;
      record `cleanup_confirmed`.

### Phase 9 completion gate

- [ ] Final rendered-screen evidence proves the requested visual behavior; unit
      layout assertions alone are not promoted to visual success.
- [ ] Final installed-runtime evidence proves exact input consumption,
      completion, Esc abort, delegation, callback reentry, reload safety, and
      cleanup without inferred results.
- [ ] The independent review reports no unmet checklist item or unexplained
      divergence from the approved contract.

## Definition of done

- [ ] `reqscript('dwarfuicore/services')` exports immutable
      `TooltipServiceProvider`, `ContextMenuServiceProvider`, and
      `UserPromptServiceProvider` objects and no unapproved provider.
- [ ] `UserPromptServiceProvider:new(1, namespace)` returns a distinct immutable
      namespace-bound API over one process-wide UserPrompt backend.
- [ ] `prompt_map_location()` accepts exactly the approved title, message,
      source, completion, and cancellation contract and returns an opaque
      namespace-owned handle.
- [ ] Only one prompt is pending process-wide; concurrent requests are rejected
      atomically and never queued or replaced.
- [ ] While pending, `_MOUSE_L_DOWN` always consumes without sampling or
      completing.
- [ ] While pending, `_MOUSE_L` always consumes and completes with exactly one
      synchronously sampled detached `{x, y, z}|nil` result without conditions.
- [ ] While pending, Esc always consumes and aborts; right-click is not treated
      as cancellation.
- [ ] All unowned input delegates unchanged through one shared reload-safe input
      router, and existing context-menu input behavior remains intact.
- [ ] The title/message window follows below the pointer through the shared
      completed-render transport, clamps to the screen, and remains
      input-transparent.
- [ ] Prompt state, input consumption, and presentation clear before terminal
      callback dispatch on every path.
- [ ] Completion/cancellation callbacks are exact-once, mutually exclusive,
      failure-contained, and safely reentrant.
- [ ] Handles enforce namespace, service, contract-major, and generation
      ownership; APIs expose no private diagnostics.
- [ ] Namespace cleanup, source invalidation, world unload, and Core reload
      leave no hidden prompt, active consumer, stale renderer, or duplicate hook.
- [ ] Tooltip and context-menu unit, live, render, lifecycle, reload, and package
      contracts remain green after sharing input/render infrastructure.
- [ ] Every new/modified class and method has LuaDoc, all closed discriminators
      are immutable numeric enums, and all production modules follow project
      loading and registry conventions.
- [ ] Focused unit, complete unit, syntax, offline build, expanded package,
      archive, focused live, complete live, rendered-screen, installed-runtime,
      reload, cleanup, and diff evidence pass as distinct gates.
- [ ] The required follow-up reviews and final independent review confirm the
      implementation fulfills this checklist before it is marked complete.

